.TH "WEBTRANSPORTDATAGRAMSWRITABLE.SENDORDER" "3JS" "September 1, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
WebTransportDatagramsWritable.sendOrder \-
WebTransportDatagramsWritable: sendOrder property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Secure context:\f[R] This feature is available only in secure
contexts (HTTPS).
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]sendOrder\f[B]\f[R] property of the
\f[CR]WebTransportDatagramsWritable\f[R] interface represents the
priority of this stream\(cqs datagrams relative to other streams and
datagrams in the same \f[CR]sendGroup\f[R], as an integer.
.PP
Within a \f[CR]sendGroup\f[R], bytes queued for sending on streams and
datagrams with a higher \f[CR]sendOrder\f[R] are sent before any bytes
from lower\-priority ones.
Different groups are expected to be treated as equals for the purposes
of bandwidth allocation \(em though the precise way bandwidth is divided
between groups is implementation\-defined.
.SH VALUE
An integer indicating the relative priority of this stream\(cqs
datagrams when sending bytes.
The default value is \f[CR]0\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
The example below shows how you can set the initial \f[CR]sendOrder\f[R]
when calling \f[CR]createWritable()\f[R] to create the writable stream,
read the value back from the stream, and then change it:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] writable = transport.datagrams.createWritable({
  sendOrder: 1, \f[I]// Set initial send order\f[R]
});

console.log(\(gaSend order: ${writable.sendOrder}\(ga); \f[I]// Send order: 1\f[R]

\f[B]const\f[R] writer = writable.getWriter();
\f[B]const\f[R] data = \f[B]new\f[R] Uint8Array([65, 66, 67]);
\f[B]await\f[R] writer.ready;
writer.write(data).catch(() \f[B]=>\f[R] {});

\f[I]// Increase the priority of this stream\(aqs datagrams\f[R]
writable.sendOrder = 2;
console.log(\(gaSend order: ${writable.sendOrder}\(ga); \f[I]// Send order: 2\f[R]
.EE
